WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.633 --> 00:00:12.053
The line between imagination and reality has long

00:00:12.053 --> 00:00:15.765
been the realm of cinematic
and literary exploration.

00:00:15.849 --> 00:00:19.352
But now, thanks to research
published in 2023,

00:00:19.436 --> 00:00:21.312
Scientists are gaining new insights

00:00:21.312 --> 00:00:24.899
into one of the most mysterious
transitions in human consciousness -

00:00:25.066 --> 00:00:30.488
the shift from reality based perception
to internal, imaginative brain states.

00:00:30.572 --> 00:00:33.533
Imagination really allows you to come up with what things

00:00:33.533 --> 00:00:37.245
would be like in alternative realities,
which is something that's really powerful.

00:00:37.245 --> 00:00:38.997
It can help you plan things.

00:00:38.997 --> 00:00:40.248
You can imagine, okay,

00:00:40.248 --> 00:00:42.876
if I'm going to have this dinner,
what's what is it going to taste like?

00:00:42.876 --> 00:00:43.918
If that's something I like?

00:00:43.918 --> 00:00:46.504
Okay, well, then let me go
and make that for dinner, for example.

00:00:46.504 --> 00:00:48.840
So it allows you to really like, plan
your future.

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:52.052
it's just interesting to know
how your brain distinguishes internally

00:00:52.052 --> 00:00:55.430
generated content from stuff
that is out there in the world.

00:00:55.555 --> 00:00:59.350
But scientists have long struggled
to understand the puzzling neuroscience

00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:03.396
behind our ability to distinguish
between perception and imagination,

00:01:03.605 --> 00:01:07.442
since both use
many of the same parts of the brain.

00:01:07.525 --> 00:01:09.861
in 1910, psychologist Mary

00:01:09.861 --> 00:01:13.865
Perky was trying to develop
a scientific definition of imagination.

00:01:13.948 --> 00:01:17.827
So she devised an experiment
that produced a surprising result.

00:01:17.911 --> 00:01:22.332
Test subjects were asked to stare at
a blank wall and imagine a certain fruit.

00:01:22.415 --> 00:01:26.711
Meanwhile, behind the scenes, Perky
projected a very faint image of the fruit

00:01:26.711 --> 00:01:27.545
on the wall.

00:01:27.545 --> 00:01:31.216
When asked if they saw anything real,
the participants were sure they hadn't,

00:01:31.216 --> 00:01:34.552
although they commented on how vivid
the image they were imagining

00:01:34.552 --> 00:01:35.826
seemed.

00:01:35.826 --> 00:01:41.309
Perky concluded that
when our perception of something matches what we're imagining,
we will assume we've imagined it.

00:01:41.476 --> 00:01:44.979
This would eventually come to be known
as the Perky effect.

00:01:45.063 --> 00:01:48.858
Since then there's been a handful
of studies that tried to replicate it,

00:01:48.858 --> 00:01:51.945
but the results were very mixed,
Nonetheless, decades kind of with

00:01:51.945 --> 00:01:55.323
the advancement of neuroimaging, we've shown time and time again

00:01:55.323 --> 00:01:58.326
that when we imagine something
many of the same brain

00:01:58.576 --> 00:02:02.664
processes becoming involved as when
we would actually see that same thing.

00:02:02.747 --> 00:02:06.876
So Dykstra and her team designed
an updated version of Perky's experiment.

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:08.169
The setup was similar.

00:02:08.169 --> 00:02:11.548
But to better control for the variability
in imagined objects,

00:02:11.714 --> 00:02:15.260
the researchers asked participants
to imagine something more specific:

00:02:15.343 --> 00:02:17.095
diagonal lines.

00:02:17.095 --> 00:02:20.390
Participants stared at a static screen
and were asked to imagine

00:02:20.390 --> 00:02:23.393
the lines, just as in Perky's experiment,

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:26.258
faint images would appear and disappear.

00:02:26.258 --> 00:02:29.225
What we found was actually kind of surprising

00:02:29.225 --> 00:02:31.526
because we did not find a Perky effect.

00:02:31.693 --> 00:02:32.735
instead,

00:02:32.735 --> 00:02:35.488
in results published in 2023, a more

00:02:35.488 --> 00:02:38.491
nuanced picture emerged.
When the researchers

00:02:38.533 --> 00:02:42.162
asked participants if the lines they saw
were real or imagined,

00:02:42.328 --> 00:02:43.705
the responses varied.

00:02:43.705 --> 00:02:46.332
When they were shown faint
images of the lines.

00:02:46.332 --> 00:02:49.085
Participants
were more likely to report seeing them,

00:02:49.085 --> 00:02:51.045
and when they weren't shown the lines.

00:02:51.045 --> 00:02:55.341
some still reported seeing them
suggesting vivid imaginations at work.

00:02:55.425 --> 00:02:59.262
So taken together,
what we can kind of conclude from this is

00:02:59.262 --> 00:03:03.850
that when imagination gets really vivid,
it can be mistaken for reality.

00:03:03.933 --> 00:03:05.685
so the way that we are thinking about this

00:03:05.685 --> 00:03:09.731
now is that
this is all to do with sensory strength.

00:03:09.814 --> 00:03:13.443
Unlike perky, Dykstra went beyond
first person reporting.

00:03:13.526 --> 00:03:17.655
She and her collaborators analyzed
neuroimaging data from the participants

00:03:17.655 --> 00:03:18.698
in their study.

00:03:18.698 --> 00:03:23.411
What they saw suggests that the visual
and prefrontal cortices may be directly

00:03:23.411 --> 00:03:27.749
involved in the process of distinguishing
imagination from reality.

00:03:27.832 --> 00:03:32.212
So I intuitively always thought
it was going to be more like a switch.

00:03:32.212 --> 00:03:37.800
But the research seems to suggest that
it might be more like a gradual meter.

00:03:37.884 --> 00:03:41.888
But then the way you're interpreting
this is more binary.

00:03:41.888 --> 00:03:46.059
So like once it crosses some threshold,
you're like, now it's real,

00:03:46.142 --> 00:03:47.602
and before that it's not real.

00:03:47.602 --> 00:03:50.480
And that point is what we call
the reality threshold.

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:53.316
And they're basically saying
that the way that we distinguish

00:03:53.316 --> 00:03:58.154
what is seen versus what is imagined,
that strength of this representation.

00:03:58.238 --> 00:04:01.783
has to exceed the threshold
for us to consider it real

00:04:01.783 --> 00:04:03.201
or out there in the world.

00:04:03.201 --> 00:04:04.661
And that's something
that we're really keen

00:04:04.661 --> 00:04:07.664
to investigate further,
whether we can get measures of this

00:04:07.747 --> 00:04:10.500
in individual people
and then see if that relates to things

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:14.504
like hallucination proneness
or schizophrenia or sometimes

00:04:14.504 --> 00:04:18.341
in Parkinson's, people experience
these really vivid hallucinations

00:04:18.341 --> 00:04:21.886
that are actually imaginations,
but they feel real to these people.

00:04:21.970 --> 00:04:25.723
So one hypothesis would be that
something is wrong in their reality

00:04:25.723 --> 00:04:27.558
threshold the more I learn,

00:04:27.558 --> 00:04:30.697
the more questions I have
and the more I want to keep going.

00:04:33.815 --> 00:04:37.402
Humans
are made of nearly 70 trillion cells,

00:04:37.485 --> 00:04:39.904
but only half of them are truly ours.

00:04:39.904 --> 00:04:44.951
The other half are foreign microbes,
collectively called the human microbiome.

00:04:45.034 --> 00:04:46.786
Covering us inside and out,

00:04:46.786 --> 00:04:51.457
this intricate ecosystem helps
us digest food and fight off disease.

00:04:51.541 --> 00:04:54.711
Even though the microbiome
is an essential part of who we are,

00:04:54.794 --> 00:04:57.380
it's still largely a mystery to science.

00:04:57.380 --> 00:04:58.631
Researchers have been trying

00:04:58.631 --> 00:05:01.801
to better understand
how we acquire these foreign microbes

00:05:01.926 --> 00:05:04.178
and if they're exchanged freely
between people

00:05:04.178 --> 00:05:05.805
like other germs.

00:05:05.805 --> 00:05:08.641
So we say microbiome, but this means

00:05:08.641 --> 00:05:12.478
many different species,
hundreds to thousands.

00:05:12.562 --> 00:05:15.481
So it's not just studying one pathogen,

00:05:15.481 --> 00:05:19.902
we have to study so many microorganisms,
all of them are different

00:05:20.069 --> 00:05:24.324
These organisms vary in terms
of their genomes, their functions,

00:05:24.449 --> 00:05:27.910
what they're doing, why they're there,
how they interface with the human body.

00:05:27.910 --> 00:05:30.580
And that presents
a lot of different challenges

00:05:30.580 --> 00:05:34.792
how to grow these organisms, how to study
their different and various functions.

00:05:34.792 --> 00:05:38.087
There's another reason
our microbiome is tricky to study.

00:05:38.171 --> 00:05:40.882
Its inhabitants are constantly changing.

00:05:40.882 --> 00:05:43.885
At birth we have an imprinting
of the bacteria from the

00:05:44.052 --> 00:05:44.844
and from the environment.

00:05:44.844 --> 00:05:49.265
And then we continuously update,
improve, change,

00:05:49.265 --> 00:05:53.478
modify
our microbiome during all our lifetime.

00:05:53.561 --> 00:05:56.856
This year, a team of biologists
at the University of Trento

00:05:56.856 --> 00:05:59.233
in Italy developed new ways to trace

00:05:59.233 --> 00:06:03.112
genetic strains of microbes
as they pass between individuals.

00:06:03.196 --> 00:06:08.242
So we try to collect as many samples
as possible to get a global understanding

00:06:08.242 --> 00:06:12.413
of microbiome transmission
in the different lifestyles in the planet.

00:06:12.622 --> 00:06:18.002
We collected more than 9000 samples
for the gut and the microbiome,

00:06:18.002 --> 00:06:23.091
so stool and saliva samples
in more than 30 different countries.

00:06:23.174 --> 00:06:26.260
We just sequence
all the DNA that's there in the sample.

00:06:26.427 --> 00:06:30.515
The challenge here is to make sense
of the sequencing data, considering that

00:06:30.515 --> 00:06:35.812
we are not looking at one individual but
a community of individuals all together.

00:06:35.895 --> 00:06:39.732
Comparing the individual's data
across familial and social networks,

00:06:39.941 --> 00:06:43.611
the researchers wanted to know
if microorganisms in the microbiome

00:06:43.694 --> 00:06:47.740
could spread from person to person,
like a cold or a stomach bug.

00:06:47.824 --> 00:06:52.120
And what they found suggests that microbes
hop much more extensively

00:06:52.120 --> 00:06:53.496
than we thought between people

00:06:53.496 --> 00:06:57.917
in close physical proximity,
like roommates, spouses and children.

00:06:58.084 --> 00:07:03.381
The power of our study was really
that what we saw was consistent

00:07:03.464 --> 00:07:06.467
across all these populations
and different lifestyles.

00:07:06.676 --> 00:07:09.595
There is really a massive transmission
of human

00:07:09.595 --> 00:07:12.682
microbiome members
directly through social interactions.

00:07:12.682 --> 00:07:17.061
So our social activity impacts
our microbiome,

00:07:17.061 --> 00:07:18.855
which in turn impact our biology.

00:07:18.855 --> 00:07:22.733
So, the way and the people we interact
with is impacting

00:07:22.817 --> 00:07:25.778
our biology, our medical history.

00:07:25.778 --> 00:07:30.158
I think it warrants additional study
into the dynamics and the true sources

00:07:30.158 --> 00:07:33.911
and the full range of sources
where these organisms come from.

00:07:34.078 --> 00:07:37.039
But this is a huge step in

00:07:37.039 --> 00:07:40.001
understanding the transmission landscape.

00:07:40.042 --> 00:07:43.296
As humans evolved,
our microbiomes may have evolved

00:07:43.296 --> 00:07:46.299
with us
to protect our health and immunity.

00:07:46.340 --> 00:07:50.761
But with the continued use of antibiotics
and antiseptics in the modern world,

00:07:50.761 --> 00:07:55.391
we may be throwing off our body's natural
microbial balance and leaving ourselves.

00:07:55.391 --> 00:07:57.810
more susceptible to the health risks.

00:07:57.810 --> 00:08:01.564
The more we study it, the more we realize
that the microbiome underlies

00:08:01.647 --> 00:08:05.151
a lot of different immune
related disorders in the body.

00:08:05.151 --> 00:08:09.906
So many of these are chronic conditions,
things like cancers, autoimmunity

00:08:09.906 --> 00:08:11.449
inflammatory bowel disease

00:08:11.449 --> 00:08:15.244
and diabetes, multiple sclerosis
and many other disorders.

00:08:15.244 --> 00:08:20.249
And so we typically didn't understand
the root causes of those diseases.

00:08:20.249 --> 00:08:21.626
But as we explore them more,

00:08:21.626 --> 00:08:23.961
they're not only genetic
and environmental,

00:08:23.961 --> 00:08:26.964
but they have a lot to do with
these organisms that live within our body.

00:08:27.006 --> 00:08:29.842
We cannot fully infer causality,

00:08:29.842 --> 00:08:31.177
so we don't know

00:08:31.177 --> 00:08:35.306
if the disease occurs first of them
or the microbiome alterations.

00:08:35.515 --> 00:08:38.726
And so it's very important
to us to understand

00:08:38.726 --> 00:08:45.921
how we acquired this bacteria and how
we transmit them to other individuals.

00:08:48.027 --> 00:08:50.279
This is a human embryo.

00:08:50.279 --> 00:08:52.281
This is a mouse embryo.

00:08:52.281 --> 00:08:55.952
They both start growing
and building a body in much the same way.

00:08:56.035 --> 00:08:58.287
But then something strange happens,

00:08:58.287 --> 00:09:04.043
the human embryo's development slows down
and the mouse's accelerates.

00:09:04.126 --> 00:09:09.382
As far as we can tell, every step
takes about 2 to 3 times

00:09:09.382 --> 00:09:14.262
longer in the human embryo than a
in the mouse embryo.

00:09:14.345 --> 00:09:18.474
How and when different embryonic tissues
develop can dramatically alter

00:09:18.474 --> 00:09:21.561
an organism's form.
But a mystery persists.

00:09:21.769 --> 00:09:25.314
What controls this difference in timing?

00:09:25.398 --> 00:09:28.776
New evidence suggests
the mysterious conductor driving

00:09:28.776 --> 00:09:33.322
development is an organelle scientists
may have written off as a one trick pony.

00:09:33.406 --> 00:09:36.867
So when people think about mitochondria,
traditionally,

00:09:36.951 --> 00:09:39.787
they think about the powerhouse
of the cell,

00:09:39.787 --> 00:09:44.417
which is producing ATP to provide energy
for studying our functions.

00:09:44.500 --> 00:09:47.545
But they're also involved in signaling,

00:09:47.545 --> 00:09:51.882
there is a lot more going on in
mitochondria than just energy production.

00:09:51.966 --> 00:09:54.719
To figure out the causal relationship
between activity

00:09:54.719 --> 00:09:58.014
in the mitochondria
and the timing of embryonic development.

00:09:58.055 --> 00:10:01.517
researchers at Harvard Medical School,
manipulated different regions

00:10:01.517 --> 00:10:05.021
within the mitochondria of human and mouse
stem cells.

00:10:05.104 --> 00:10:08.649
and in early 2023,
they published their results.

00:10:08.733 --> 00:10:12.945
So we observe that mouse cells produce
protein faster

00:10:12.945 --> 00:10:17.033
through the process
of mRNA translation than human cells.

00:10:17.033 --> 00:10:22.663
And we found that if we experimentally
increased the activity of mitochondria,

00:10:22.663 --> 00:10:28.669
we could also force the cells to produce
protein faster and develop faster.

00:10:28.753 --> 00:10:32.465
This finding was then echoed by
a team of researchers working in Belgium

00:10:32.465 --> 00:10:36.552
in a paper they published in Science
just weeks later, which pointed towards

00:10:36.552 --> 00:10:40.056
the mitochondria's role in developmental
timing in the nervous system.

00:10:40.139 --> 00:10:43.059
Mitochondria is important in many aspects.

00:10:43.059 --> 00:10:46.854
Just not only the ATP protein
and the metabolite production.

00:10:47.063 --> 00:10:51.400
And the what we found is a mitochondria
is a working as a pacemaker of the

00:10:51.609 --> 00:10:55.571
speed of the development
in cells. I think very important

00:10:55.571 --> 00:10:59.200
next question is how
mitochondrial activity regulate

00:10:59.367 --> 00:11:02.620
gene regulatory network
because gene regulatory network

00:11:02.620 --> 00:11:07.166
is also important to regulate the timing
of that development.

00:11:07.249 --> 00:11:10.252
This research could have
major implications for cell therapy,

00:11:10.336 --> 00:11:14.590
which is when damaged cells are replaced
by healthy cells, and cancer research.

00:11:14.674 --> 00:11:18.302
If we can find a way
by setting developmental speed

00:11:18.344 --> 00:11:22.515
to accelerate the production
of cells for therapies, then that would be

00:11:22.515 --> 00:11:26.894
really important in accelerating
the cell therapy revolution.

00:11:27.019 --> 00:11:30.439
Tempo is important
to make up different outcome.

00:11:30.523 --> 00:11:31.440
That's why

00:11:31.440 --> 00:11:33.109
mitochondria is that important

00:11:33.109 --> 00:11:37.571
pacemaker and the make
a diversity of our animal kingdom.

00:11:37.571 --> 00:11:42.034
So they have out this kind of as a function as a driving force of the evolution.

